Australia
so obviously sun protection is one of my

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.130
big obsessions a good sunscreen is one
of the most important things you can use

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.900
to prevent pigmentation so things like
sunspots and to help you fade acne marks

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.670
to understand sunscreen you need to know
about UVA and so that's the topic of

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.690
today's video if you like this sort of
video give it a thumbs up subscribe to

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.140
my channel and make sure you hit the
notification button so that you don't

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:54.090
miss out on any videos so first up what
is UVA the Sun emits 3 main types of

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.890
light there's UV which is ultraviolet
there's visible light which we can see

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:04.019
and there's infrared or IR I've talked
about what visible light can do to your

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.130
skin in a previous video and today we're
going to be focusing on the Big Kahuna

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:14.640
which is UV UV light is divided into 3
major types this is according to how

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.350
much energy they have which is inversely
proportional to their wavelength in

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.490
other words the shorter the wavelength
the higher the energy the shortest

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:30.060
wavelength highest energy light is UVC
this has wavelengths of 100 to 280

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.220
nanometers since it's the most energetic
it's the most harmful luckily it's also

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.150
the least penetrating it's almost
completely blocked by the atmosphere and

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.079
the ozone layer so you don't really need
to worry too much about it the next type

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:50.460
is UVB which is from 280 nanometers to
350 nanometers this is probably the type

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.649
of UV you've heard about the most about
90% of the UVB that the Sun produces

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:59.520
gets absorbed by the ozone layer which
is 25 kilometers above the Earth's

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.509
surface this means that for Australians
and other people who live close to the

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.950
South Pole it's a bigger issue because
there's an ozone hole above Antarctica

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:12.780
which means that it lets in more UV
light we also have really nice sunny

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.320
weather which means
we're more likely to be outside uvb causes

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.630
sunburns and skin cancer but luckily
it is mostly blocked by glass it's also

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.520
at the type of UV from the Sun that's
involved in vitamin D production UVB is

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.840
the strongest in summer and in the
middle of the day it's mostly what's

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.620
measured by the UV index which you can
look up on your local weather site

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:41.610
finally we have UVA which is from about
350 to 400 nanometers this is the lowest

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.910
energy since it has the longest
wavelength out of the different types of

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:49.590
UV light it has slightly shorter
wavelengths than visible light UVA

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.570
penetrates the atmosphere better it's
about 95 percent of the UV that reaches

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.010
the Earth's surface and it also
penetrates deeper into your skin than

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.410
UVB the main impact of UVA on your skin
is that it forms highly reactive free

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.700
radicals in your skin which have
unpaired electrons this means that

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.570
they're really good at reacting with
whatever's around like your DNA

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:17.310
UVA causes wrinkles and sun damage in
your skin as well as deadly melanomas

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:22.739
it's the type of UV that you'll find in
tanning beds and in black lights like

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.870
with UVB UVA levels depend on the time
of day and the time of year but compared

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:32.160
to UVB it varies a lot less that means
it's important to think about UVA

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.980
protection even during off-peak times as
well UVA is further subdivided into uva1

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.739
and uva2. uva1 has longer
wavelengths so it's above 340 nanometers

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:50.190
uva2 has shorter wavelengths it's
closer to UVB and it's below 340

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:56.040
nanometers the main reason that UVA is
less famous than UVB is that the dangers

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.840
of UVA weren't really known until
relatively recently UVA effects are also

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.459
less immediate and they build up over
time which means that it's sort of more

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:11.580
dangerous UVA's ability to penetrate
deeper than UVB is also quite scary so

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.239
can penetrate through glass like the
glass in your car side windows which is

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:19.290
why there's lots of asymmetric skin
cancers emerging as well as asymmetric

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.820
wrinkle patterns so for example there's
a famous photo of a truck driver he

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.300
drove a truck with the windows closed
for about 28 years

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.259
the left side of his face received
direct Sun the right side received

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:35.879
indirect Sun and you can see the massive
difference in wrinkles even if you never

00:04:35.879 --> 00:04:40.530
go to the beach and you never sunbathe
you can still be affected by UVA it's

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.259
been estimated that about 80% of UVA
damage actually comes from everyday life

00:04:45.259 --> 00:04:49.680
so things like if you're walking around
if you're driving or even if you're

00:04:49.680 --> 00:05:00.000
sitting near a window now let's talk
about sunscreen sunscreens almost always

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.330
have an SPF rating SPF stands for Sun
Protection factor and the higher the SPF

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:10.680
the higher the protection you have
against erythemal UV in other words UV

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.560
that causes your skin to burn SPF mostly
measures protection against UVB although

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.909
shorter wavelengths of UVA will also
cause burning about 9 to 20% of the

00:05:20.909 --> 00:05:25.740
reaction if you have a sunscreen that
only blocks against UVB you can actually

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:32.190
only get an SPF of 11 so there is UVA
protection shown in the SPF the concept

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:37.919
of SPF was introduced in 1970 before all
of this UVA research and so back then

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:41.759
scientists assumed that the only damage
you could get to your skin was sunburn

00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:45.900
and so that's what we protected
ourselves against using sunscreens I've

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.680
talked more about SPF and whether or not
higher SPFs are better in a different

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.099
video which I've linked below the
problem with SPF is that it doesn't

00:05:53.099 --> 00:05:56.430
really tell us that much about
protection against longer wavelengths of

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.979
UV especially that UVA1 because we
now know that UVA is also dangerous a

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:06.000
lot of countries have introduced other
regulated terms in sunscreens that

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.409
describe how much UVA protection you're
getting the most popular term is

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:14.099
something called broad spectrum there
are a few slightly different ways of

00:06:14.099 --> 00:06:18.479
measuring it the first method is using
something called critical wavelength the

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:22.680
ability of the sunscreen to absorb UV is
measured over different wavelengths and

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.789
for a sunscreen should be classified 
broad spectrum under this critical

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:32.190
wavelength criteria 90% of the total
area under the absorption curve from 290

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:37.979
to 400 nanometers needs to be higher or
equal to 317 nanometers the other

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:40.770
criteria that sometimes used for
broad spectrum

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:45.780
is that the UVA protection factor has to
be at least one third of the labeled SPF

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.280
in Australia in the EU both of these
have to happen before a sunscreen gets

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.840
the broad spectrum rating in the u.s.
broad spectrum sunscreen only needs the

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.610
critical wavelength criteria what this
means is that in general a higher SPF is

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:05.550
going to give you better UVA protection
Australian and EU sunscreens also give

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:09.720
you better guaranteed protection than a
u.s. sunscreen that's because critical

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.440
wavelength does tell you about how wide
the protection is so the broad spectrum

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.550
but it doesn't tell you that much info
about how much protection you're getting

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:22.620
so in other words how strong that
protection is one recent study did find

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.699
that US sunscreens give less protection
than the sunscreens that require both

00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:31.830
critical wavelengths and the one third
SPF rule out of the 20 sunscreens tested

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:37.710
in this study 19 of them passed the US
test but only 11 of them passed the EU

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:42.750
regulations with the extra 1/3 SPF
requirement in some other countries

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.690
there's more specific UVA protection
ratings

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:50.699
there's the PPD rating which stands for
persistent pigment darkening this

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:54.449
measures how well a sunscreen protects
you against a long-term tan that's

00:07:54.449 --> 00:07:59.880
caused by UVA it's basically the
equivalent of SPF but for UVA you'll

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:05.610
often find PPD ratings in European
sunscreens there's also the PA rating

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:10.830
this is pretty similar to PPD but it
uses plus signs and it's more popular in

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:16.259
Asian countries pa+
roughly translates to PPD 2 to 4, pa

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:22.529
double plus is PPD four to eight Pa
triple plus is PPD eight to sixteen and

00:08:22.529 --> 00:08:29.340
PA quadruple plus is PPD 16 and above
unfortunately PA ratings don't tell you

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.610
how far above PPD 16 a sunscreen might
be

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:42.129
another way of checking if you're
getting good UVA protection is by

00:08:42.129 --> 00:08:46.420
looking at the actual sunscreen
ingredients most sunscreen ingredients

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.769
don't give great protection against
longer wavelength UVA1 these curves

00:08:50.769 --> 00:08:55.809
show you the protection ranges a higher
line means higher protection but higher

00:08:55.809 --> 00:08:59.110
percentages of that sunscreen ingredient
will give you high protection so the

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:02.350
curve will go up so in terms of the
relative protection it doesn't tell you

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:07.149
that much but it does tell you the
breadth of protection the best uva1

00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:12.069
protective ingredients for a long time
were avobenzone and zinc oxide

00:09:12.069 --> 00:09:18.129
avobenzone or BMDBM gives really good
protection but it's photounstable which

00:09:18.129 --> 00:09:22.329
means it breaks down in UV light so if
it's on your skin it might stop

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:26.679
protecting you after a while unless it's
being stabilized zinc oxide gives you

00:09:26.679 --> 00:09:30.639
pretty flat protection throughout the
different wavelengths but the problem is

00:09:30.639 --> 00:09:35.079
it has really low protection which means
that in a sunscreen what you need is a

00:09:35.079 --> 00:09:39.309
really high percentage to lift that
curve up and the problem with high

00:09:39.309 --> 00:09:43.179
percentages is that a lot of the time
the sunscreen feels gross and it gives

00:09:43.179 --> 00:09:47.139
you a white cast you can see the white
cast from these three sunscreens that

00:09:47.139 --> 00:09:49.949
I've tried in the past

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.810
there are now some newest sunscreen
ingredients that aren't really that new

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.770
but they give better protection and they
avoid these issues with avobenzone

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.310
and zinc oxide they're longer-lasting
because they're photostable

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:06.090
so they don't break down in UV and
they're really effective so you don't

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:09.650
need to have too much in a sunscreen to
give you that high protection

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:14.100
unfortunately most of these aren't
available in the US unless you import

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:19.050
sunscreen from overseas these are the
best longer-wavelength UVA protective

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:26.070
ingredients there's tinosorb s which is
abbreviated as BEMT tinosorb M which is

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:32.250
MBBT Mexoryl XL which is DTS this
is patented by L'Oreal and it's found

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:38.790
in La roche-posay products there's also
uvinul a plus or DHHB mexoryl SX

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:45.840
or TDSA and there's Neo Heliopan AP
or DPDT some of the older more

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.370
traditional sunscreen ingredients will
also block against UVA too so for

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:56.220
example oxybenzone octocrylene and
titanium dioxide how much protection you

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:59.640
get also depends a lot on the
formulation so make sure you check the

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:05.160
regulated terms as well so the SPF the
PPD the words broad-spectrum as well as

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:08.910
looking for these ingredients if you're
interested in learning more about how to

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.850
pick a good sunscreen
I've written a skincare guide which I've

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.270
linked below it also talks about how to
pick a good cleanser and a good

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.550
moisturizer and how to set up a skincare
routine

00:11:21.959 --> 00:11:27.010
as you can probably guess by now
sunscreens aren't the best type of sun

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:31.450
protection a lot of them don't have the
best UVA protection you need to reapply

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:37.180
them regularly and it's really easy to
miss a spot or apply too little but we

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:41.170
do have some other options as well some
protective clothing is a really good

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.740
option so things like hats and
sunglasses and shirts hats are really

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:49.000
good for protecting your scalp which is
an area that most of us tend to miss you

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.589
should also try to stay in the shade and
avoid sun exposure at peak times UV

00:11:53.589 --> 00:11:57.339
that's been reflected off the surface
onto you will still cause damage but a

00:11:57.339 --> 00:12:01.600
lot less than the direct UV so you can
see that from the truck-driver photo you

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.740
can also look into adding uva tints on
windows that you go near a lot you don't

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:08.260
need to worry too much about the
windshield because they're already

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:12.490
designed to block UVA I hope you like
